\title{Soundness Notions}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-19}
\meta{toc}{true}

\p{
    Soundness is the claim that a cheating prover cannot make the verifier accept a false statement, except with small probability. [Completeness](dhsorens-004T) is the matching guarantee for an honest prover with a true statement, and is almost never where a protocol fails. The definition has to name the adversary: which provers, in which game, with which oracles.
}

\p{
    \em{Sound} names a family of properties. Most papers prove the weakest member; [compilation](dhsorens-004S) (interactive protocol to deployed non-interactive argument) needs a stronger one. Khovratovich, Rothblum, and Soukhanov [proved false statements](dhsorens-0057) against a deployed [GKR-based argument](dhsorens-002Q) whose interactive soundness was never in question. The interactive theorem was true; it was the wrong theorem.
}

\p{
    The notions, weakest first, for a [public-coin](dhsorens-0053) protocol #{\Pi} with #{\mu} challenge rounds deciding membership in a language #{L}. Each is a different quantification over [transcripts](dhsorens-0054), and each was first written down for the three-move [sigma protocol](dhsorens-0055) before being generalised.
    \ul{
        \li{\strong{Soundness} — no prover convinces the verifier of a false statement except with probability at most #{\epsilon}. Which provers are admitted is the whole content of the definition: unbounded ones make #{\Pi} a proof, computationally bounded ones an argument.}
        \li{\strong{Knowledge soundness} — an efficient extractor recovers a witness from any prover that convinces #{V}. Strictly stronger, and the only one of the two with content when every statement is in the language.}
        \li{\strong{Special soundness} — a combinatorial condition on the verifier: a tree of accepting transcripts, branching at each challenge round, yields a witness. Not a security guarantee but a hypothesis that implies one, and checkable by inspecting the algebra. Lattice protocols, whose challenges are vectors, run on a coordinate-wise variant that branches per coordinate rather than per challenge.}
        \li{\strong{Round-by-round soundness} — every transcript prefix carries a state, a false statement starts \em{doomed}, and a single round escapes doom with probability at most #{\epsilon_i}.}
        \li{\strong{State-restoration soundness} — soundness against a prover that may return to any prefix it has already built and draw a fresh challenge there, as often as its budget allows.}
    }
    Three qualifiers cut across all five and are quoted less often than they should be: whether the statement is fixed before the hash function or [chosen after](dhsorens-004O), whether the extractor may [rewind or must work straightline](dhsorens-004J), and which oracles the adversary is given. A soundness claim missing any of the three is not yet a claim.
}

\p{
    The implications make the family a hierarchy rather than a list. Knowledge soundness implies soundness once the knowledge error is below one. Round-by-round soundness implies soundness with error #{\sum_{i=1}^{\mu} \epsilon_i}, by a union bound over rounds. State-restoration soundness implies soundness with the error preserved, and is equivalent to round-by-round soundness up to a polynomial loss. Special soundness implies round-by-round soundness — Block, Garreta, Tiwari, and Zając prove this for generalised forms of both. Each notion has a knowledge counterpart, and round-by-round knowledge soundness implies both round-by-round soundness and plain knowledge soundness. [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) states these arrows as theorems in \code{OracleReduction/Security/Implications.lean} — knowledge soundness to soundness, round-by-round to plain in both directions, state restoration to both — which is a useful check on the decomposition, since each arrow has to be proved rather than asserted.
}

\p{
    [Compilation](dhsorens-004S) is where a notion gets chosen, not merely inherited. [Sequential composition](dhsorens-002I) preserves plain soundness, with the errors accumulating. The [Fiat–Shamir transform](dhsorens-002G) does not: analysed from plain soundness alone, a #{(2\mu+1)}-move protocol loses a factor #{Q^\mu} in the adversary's random-oracle query count #{Q}, which says nothing at all for the twenty-odd rounds of a folding argument. From special soundness the loss is #{Q+1} regardless of round count; from round-by-round soundness it is likewise linear in #{Q}. The [BCS transform](dhsorens-002H) is analysed against state restoration directly. A many-round protocol therefore has to be analysed round-by-round from the start, because an end-to-end soundness bound cannot be repaired after the fact.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-004J} % soundness, knowledge soundness, extraction
